  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 110 PM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A severe weather threat will remain through the \nafternoon for the eastern half of the forecast area.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has taken a small area in eastern North \nCarolina and upgraded the severe weather potential from a marginal \n(level 1 of 5) to a slight (level 2 of 5) risk, primarily along and \nto the east of I-95. This is primarily for an increased threat of \ndamaging winds, although large hail or an isolated tornado also \ncannot be ruled out. However, the window for severe weather in the \nRaleigh forecast area is closing, as current radar trends suggest \nthat thunderstorms should move east of the forecast area between 3 \nand 5pm.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... After today, the weather will turn dry again for \nseveral days. Temperatures will be below normal this weekend, \nwarming up to above normal for next week.\n\nHigh pressure will be dominating the weather for the next several \ndays across the southeastern United States. With very little \nmoisture, skies should be mostly clear for multiple days. While \nhighs will be in the low to mid 60s on Saturday and Sunday, highs \nwill rise into the upper 60s and 70s for Monday, Tuesday, and \nWednesday. The coldest night will be Saturday night, with \ntemperatures ranging from the mid 30s to the mid 40s, and some frost \nhas been added to the weather grids for early Sunday morning. The \nwind should be light, but not calm, and there should also be some \npassing high clouds that night, both of which would help to reduce \nthe chance of additional radiational cooling. Later shifts will have \nthe opportunity to evaluate whether a frost advisory needs to be \nissued.\n\nThere is a bit of uncertainty as to what will go on with the pattern \nfor the end of next week. The current forecast brings in a slight \nchance of rain Thursday night and Friday, but models differ as to \nhow large of a storm system may develop along the Atlantic \ncoastline. This part of the forecast is likely to change.",
